The schema is a MarineTraffic AIS vessel-position stream. I identify the following entity structure.

Entity discovery:
1. Vessel — each record describes a ship identified by mmsi (plus imo, ship_id, callsign as secondary identifiers). Maps to bsm:Vessel. Since multiple distinct identifier schemes exist (mmsi, imo, callsign, ship_id), the AlternateIdentifier class is genuinely evidenced (this is the "several columns naming distinct schemes" case), so imo and callsign become their own AlternateIdentifier entities linked via bsm:hasAlternateIdentifier; ship_id becomes the literal bsm:sourceIdentifier.
2. Observation — each record is a timestamped AIS report; bsm:Vehicle declares bsm:hasObservation and bsm:Observation has bsm:observationDateTime matching the timestamp column. Its subject must include both mmsi and timestamp to stay distinct per report.
3. Location and AbsolutePoint — lat/lon vary per report, so a Location (via inherited bsm:hasGeographicLocation on the Observation) with bsm:hasGeometry to a bsm:AbsolutePoint carrying bsm:latitudeDecimal/bsm:longitudeDecimal.
4. Velocity and Orientation — the unattached bsm:hasVelocity / bsm:hasOrientation fit naturally on the Observation: speed→bsm:speedMetersPerSecond (unit caveat: AIS speed is not m/s, noted in confidence), course→bsm:absoluteBearingAngle, heading→bsm:headingAngle. heading vs course is the classic near-synonym split: heading matches headingAngle exactly, course is the bearing.
5. GeopoliticalAffiliation — flag ('PL') is a country code; Vessel (an Object) has bsm:hasAffiliation; ship_country is redundant and has no separate home (GeopoliticalAffiliation is not shown as a BattlespaceConcept, so no conceptName there).

Near-synonym decisions: type_name→bsm:typeName (exact match, not vesselCategoryCode); shiptype (numeric AIS type code)→bsm:vesselCategoryCode (ambiguous with ais_type_summary 'Military' — I keep the code column on the code property and put ais_type_summary on bsm:additionalInformationText with low confidence); status→bsm:operatingModeCode only weakly (AIS navigational status, qualifier mismatch, low confidence); max_speed→bsm:vesselMaximumSpeed (exact qualifier match, unlike avg_speed which has no property).

Ports (current/last/next, unlocodes, ids) and voyage data (destination, eta, distances) have no object property connecting them to any entity in this ontology — Port exists as a class but nothing links a Vessel or Observation to it, so those columns stay unmapped.

IRI consistency check: every iri value's transformation string is copied character-for-character from the referenced entity's subject_transformation (vessel→affiliation/geo ids/observation; observation→location/velocity/orientation; location→abs_point). No empty transformation objects are emitted; raw literal values omit the key entirely.

